Review the following Rust function for correctness and performance, then rewrite it so that the expert sum is
deterministic and independent of how many rows are in the batch. Point out any place where a tie could be broken
differently between two runs.

```rust
use std::cmp::Ordering;

#[derive(Clone, Copy, Debug)]
pub struct Pick {
    pub expert: u32,
    pub weight: f32,
}

/// Select the top `k` experts for every row of `scores` (row-major, `rows` x `experts`),
/// add the per-expert selection bias, normalise the unbiased scores of the chosen experts
/// and scale them by `scale`.
pub fn route(
    scores: &[f32],
    bias: &[f32],
    rows: usize,
    experts: usize,
    k: usize,
    scale: f32,
) -> Vec<Vec<Pick>> {
    assert_eq!(scores.len(), rows * experts);
    assert_eq!(bias.len(), experts);
    let mut out = Vec::with_capacity(rows);
    for r in 0..rows {
        let row = &scores[r * experts..(r + 1) * experts];
        let mut order: Vec<usize> = (0..experts).collect();
        order.sort_by(|&a, &b| {
            let sa = 1.0 / (1.0 + (-row[a]).exp()) + bias[a];
            let sb = 1.0 / (1.0 + (-row[b]).exp()) + bias[b];
            sb.partial_cmp(&sa).unwrap_or(Ordering::Equal)
        });
        order.truncate(k);
        let mut picks: Vec<Pick> = order
            .iter()
            .map(|&e| Pick {
                expert: e as u32,
                weight: 1.0 / (1.0 + (-row[e]).exp()),
            })
            .collect();
        let total: f32 = picks.iter().map(|p| p.weight).sum();
        for p in picks.iter_mut() {
            p.weight = p.weight / (total + 1e-20) * scale;
        }
        out.push(picks);
    }
    out
}

/// Combine the expert outputs of one row. `outputs[i]` is the vector produced by `picks[i]`.
pub fn combine(picks: &[Pick], outputs: &[Vec<f32>], shared: &[f32]) -> Vec<f32> {
    let width = shared.len();
    let mut acc = vec![0.0f32; width];
    for (p, o) in picks.iter().zip(outputs.iter()) {
        for j in 0..width {
            acc[j] += p.weight * o[j];
        }
    }
    for j in 0..width {
        acc[j] += shared[j];
    }
    acc
}

pub fn mean_abs(v: &[f32]) -> f32 {
    if v.is_empty() {
        return 0.0;
    }
    v.iter().map(|x| x.abs()).sum::<f32>() / v.len() as f32
}
```

Also say whether `partial_cmp(..).unwrap_or(Equal)` can misbehave when a score is NaN, and how you would test the
rewritten version against the original on random inputs, including inputs with many exactly equal scores.
